("E2 · Access Engine", "Redefine 'missing' via per-category reach (monotonicity fix)", U, 5, "score,method",
 "QUESTIONS.md D6, CHECKPOINT D33. The 'missing' rule is definitionally broken, not mistuned: a hex is a gap for category c at window w iff (no c within w) AND (c is present within w for ≥80% of walkable hexes) — but reusing one window for both the reach test and the eligibility bar violates MONOTONICITY. Anything absent within 800m is absent within 400m, so a gap at 10 min must survive at 5 min; instead Manhattan hardware gaps vanish at 5 min because hardware's 400m prevalence drops to 51% and the 80% bar simply stops expecting it (D31). The rule fuses two questions that must be separated: (a) how far people normally go for category c — a property of the category; (b) whether this hex is anomalous relative to that norm — a property of the hex.\n\nFix: give each category a fixed REACH set once from revealed spacing (loci spacing already computes hex-to-nearest distance; e.g. the distance within which ≥80% of populated hexes already have one). A hex is a gap for c iff its nearest c is beyond reach(c) — no global window survives. The 80% bar only sets reach; it is never again an eligibility filter. Apply the same treatment to the 'walkable' eligibility gate (currently ≥12/15 categories present within one window): walkable iff within reach of most categories, each at its own reach.\n\nAcceptance criterion: a MONOTONICITY unit test — tightening any distance parameter (window, reach, or the eligibility bar) may only add gaps, never remove them. The current screen fails this test; the redefinition must pass it.\n\nThreat to validity, must be stated in the output: revealed spacing reflects historical SUPPLY, not demand — a category the whole city under-supplies will look like it 'naturally' spaces wide and its gaps vanish under this rule too. Contrarian review of the reach values is required before trusting them."),
