("E2 · Access Engine", "Density-class / mode-dependent thresholds", M, 5, "score,method",
 "QUESTIONS.md D7, CHECKPOINT D31. Lower Manhattan and car-dependent outer-borough areas should not share one walk window — a 10-minute walk means something different in a district built for foot traffic than in one built for cars. Cheap proxy needing no new data: scale the per-category threshold (from the sibling ticket) by residential density class. Honest version: ACS vehicle ownership per tract (table B25044), depends on an ACS vehicle-ownership ingest; Census key is set.\n\nNote the interaction with per-category thresholds: threshold(category, density_class) is one small parameterization, not two independent sweeps multiplied together — keep it deliberately small (e.g. 2–3 density tiers) to avoid overfitting a matrix nobody can audit by eye."),
("E2 · Access Engine", "Persist hex↔business network distances (hex_poi_distance)", M, 3, "score,data-model",
 "hex_access stored only counts at 5/10/15 min and discarded the Dijkstra results, so every new question (another walk time, distance to nearest, spacing, the Google coverage comparison) meant a two-minute graph recompute — and the threshold could not be swept.\n\nNow: analysis.hex_poi_distance holds every (hex, canonical business) pair within 30 min with its network distance; hex_access is DERIVED from it in SQL so the two cannot disagree. gaps, spacing and the sweep read the table. `loci score` builds it.\n\nDONE 2026-09-02 — 16.2M rows, ~3 min; derived hex_access matched the previous build row-for-row (229,133 rows; 461,421 / 1,880,500 / 4,235,014 pairs). Nearest-of-category per hex now answers in 60 ms."),
("E2 · Access Engine", "SHIP W2: the DNCI map", H, 3, "milestone",
 "Week-2 exit criterion and the first genuinely interesting artifact. Sequential palette. Sanity-check against local knowledge before proceeding to E3."),
# ------------------------------------------------------------------ E3
("E3 · Residual and Panel", "Fit the supply model", U, 5, "model",
 "DNCI ~ pop_density + median_hh_income + transit_access + commercial_zoning_capacity + land_fraction + borough FE.\n\nCheck prediction P1: the residual must have meaningful spread. If R² > 0.9 there is nothing left to explain and the thesis has no room to be true — report that honestly rather than tuning until it isn't."),
("E3 · Residual and Panel", "Moran's I + spatial error/lag model", H, 5, "model,rigor",
 "MANDATORY, not optional. Compute Moran's I on residuals; if significant (it will be), re-estimate with pysal spreg and report both.\n\nOLS standard errors on gridded urban data are wrong. Shipping a p-value without this is shipping a p-value you cannot defend."),
("E3 · Residual and Panel", "Residual extraction + opportunity score", H, 3, "model",
 "opportunity = (−u_h)⁺ × transit_access × (ResidFAR − BuiltFAR)⁺.\n\nUnderserved AND transit-connected AND room to build. Any one of the three missing disqualifies the hex."),
("E3 · Residual and Panel", "LODES WAC annual panel loader 2002–2023", H, 5, "panel,data",
 "NY state WAC files, target NAICS 4451/4461/7224/7225/8121/8123/4441/6244. Block-level, 22 vintages.\n\nBlocked on the LODES8 block-vintage verification (E1)."),
("E3 · Residual and Panel", "LODES block → hex apportionment", H, 3, "panel,grid",
 "Areal apportionment of block-level jobs onto H3 cells.\n\nDocument the proxy assumption loudly: LODES counts JOBS, not establishments. A ten-employee supermarket and a one-employee laundromat are not comparable units (threat §7.4)."),
("E3 · Residual and Panel", "Quantify pre-2020 LODES allocation bias", H, 5, "panel,risk",
 "Discovered while resolving the LODES vintage check.\n\nLODES8 puts every year on 2020 blocks, but historical years got there by ALLOCATION, not observation: when a 2010 block splits, each job is assigned to a child block at random with probability proportional to AREA share. Census: 'the allocation is a statistical process and may not result in a distribution of jobs that exactly matches the areal distribution.'\n\nArea-proportional is a poor assumption in NYC — a block splitting into a park half and a commercial half spreads jobs by area, putting employment in the park.\n\nThis is BIAS, not noise: the error concentrates where blocks were split, blocks split where development happened, and development is the outcome variable.\n\nSize the exposure, test whether H3 res-9 aggregation actually absorbs it, and if not restrict the strongest claims to 2020+ observed data. CONTEXT.md threat 7.4(b).\n\nCANCELED 2026-09-05 (CHECKPOINT D38, Linear GTM-63): hex geography retired; sizes bias in the pre-2020 LODES panel built for the E3 causal/growth chain, which already ran and returned its rejected result — no address-level equivalent."),
("E3 · Residual and Panel", "Validate LODES 2023 against establishment counts", H, 3, "panel,validation",
 "Correlate the 2023 LODES cross-section against 2023 DOHMH/DCWP establishment counts per hex. Report the correlation.\n\nThis is what licenses the jobs→establishments proxy. Without it the whole panel rests on an unexamined assumption.\n\nCANCELED 2026-09-05 (CHECKPOINT D38, Linear GTM-38): hex geography retired; validates the LODES panel for the abandoned E3 causal chain — D40's ZBP comparison now serves this role directly."),
("E3 · Residual and Panel", "Assemble outcome variables", H, 5, "outcomes,data",
 "Δlog population, Δlog households (ACS); Δlog ZORI (Zillow, ZIP→hex); permitted residential units (DOB/HPD).\n\nZIP→hex crosswalk by population weighting — CONTEXT.md open question #4. Label rent as the weakest of the four outcomes in the memo and mean it.\n\nCANCELED 2026-09-05 (CHECKPOINT D38, Linear GTM-39): hex geography retired; these outcomes fed the E3 growth regression (GTM-40), which already ran and returned the rejected null — no address-level equivalent."),
("E3 · Residual and Panel", "Main growth regression (prediction P2)", U, 5, "model,finding",
 "Δy_{h,t0→t1} = β·u_{h,t0} + γ'X_{h,t0} + α_borough + ε, t0=2013 t1=2023.\n\nP2 predicts β < 0 (more negative residual → more subsequent growth). A null or wrong-signed result is a real answer: it would mean gaps persist because they reflect durable demand suppression, not latent opportunity. Report it either way."),
("E3 · Residual and Panel", "Pre-trend test (2003→2013)", U, 3, "model,rigor",
 "Estimate the same spec on the PRIOR decade. If u_2013 also 'predicts' 2003→2013 growth, parallel trends is broken and the causal reading is unavailable.\n\nReport it whichever way it comes out. This is the check a serious reader will ask for first."),
("E3 · Residual and Panel", "Placebo outcome", H, 2, "model,rigor",
 "An outcome with no plausible mechanism — e.g. change in share of population aged 65+ — must return a null. If it doesn't, the specification is picking up generic neighborhood trajectory rather than the retail channel."),
("E3 · Residual and Panel", "MAUP sweep at res 8 and res 10", H, 3, "model,rigor",
 "Re-run the whole chain at two other resolutions; report coefficient stability. If conclusions flip with grid size, say so plainly (threat §7.3).\n\nCANCELED 2026-09-05 (CHECKPOINT D38, Linear GTM-43): hex geography retired; a hex-resolution MAUP re-gridding sweep has no address-level analogue."),
("E3 · Residual and Panel", "Tier-weight sensitivity analysis", M, 3, "model,method",
 "Tier weights (0.40/0.20/0.25/0.15) are judgment, not derived. Sweep plausible alternatives and report whether the top-20 list is stable under reweighting. CONTEXT.md open question #1.\n\nCANCELED 2026-09-05 (CHECKPOINT D38, Linear GTM-44): hex geography retired; sweeps hex DNCI/supply-model tier weights feeding the abandoned E3 residual — no address-level equivalent."),
("E3 · Residual and Panel", "Top-20 list + zoning-artifact audit", U, 3, "finding,qa",
 "Produce the ranked list of transit-rich underserved hexes.\n\nHARD GATE: manually inspect for park edges, industrial zones and cemetery blocks. Any present means the commercial-zoning control failed — fix the model, do not hand-remove the rows.\n\nAlso check it contains ≥3 genuine surprises. A list of only obvious places means the residual isn't doing any work."),
("E3 · Residual and Panel", "Spacing and nearest-missing distance diagnostics", L, 2, "model,exploratory",
 "QUESTIONS.md D5. Two read-only diagnostics the gap screen cannot answer on its own: (1) nearest-neighbour distance between businesses of the SAME category — how clustered each trade is and how often a business has no competitor within a walk; (2) for each gap hex, straight-line distance to the nearest business of its lead-missing category — separates marginal 10-minute gaps from real holes.\n\nDONE 2026-09-02 — `loci spacing` (src/loci/model/spacing.py). Found and fixed on the way: DuckDB ST_Distance_Sphere reads (lat, lon) — decision D16, shared METRES_SQL in db.py."),
("E3 · Residual and Panel", "Residual convergence test", L, 3, "panel,exploratory",
 "QUESTIONS.md T5 — does the gap close on its own?\n\nOver 2002–2023, do hexes with a negative residual at t converge toward their peers by t+k (retail employment growth regressed on the lagged residual, borough FE)?\n\nWhy it matters: if the market already corrects undersupply, the opportunity is TIMING, not location — a different product and a different memo. Cheap once the LODES panel exists; pull forward if W3 has slack, otherwise it slides to Phase 5.\n\nCANCELED 2026-09-05 (CHECKPOINT D38, Linear GTM-64): hex geography retired; belongs to the closed E3 causal program — no address-level equivalent."),
("E3 · Residual and Panel", "Retail lead/lag timing test", L, 3, "panel,exploratory",
 "QUESTIONS.md T6 — does retail lead or lag rooftops?\n\nCross-correlate annual per-hex changes in LODES retail employment with changes in population at leads and lags of 1–5 years.\n\nWhy it matters: 'retail follows rooftops' is the assumption the whole residual design rests on (D1). This is the one place it gets tested rather than asserted.\n\nCAVEATS to state: ACS 5-year smoothing limits timing resolution to roughly half-decades; LODES counts jobs, not storefronts (M2).\n\nCANCELED 2026-09-05 (CHECKPOINT D38, Linear GTM-65): hex geography retired; tests the D1 assumption underlying the abandoned hex residual design — no address-level equivalent."),
("E3 · Residual and Panel", "SHIP W3: empirical result + top-20", H, 2, "milestone",
 "Week-3 exit criterion.\n\nCANCELED 2026-09-05 (CHECKPOINT D38, Linear GTM-46): hex geography retired; ships the E3 hex residual coefficient and top-20, retained only as honest negative science, not the product. The address-level ranked list is a separate, already-shipped deliverable (D44)."),
# ------------------------------------------------------------------ E4
("E4 · Validation and Artifact", "Design stratified coverage validation sample", U, 3, "validation,critical",
 "Prediction P3 and threat §7.1 — the one that can invalidate everything.\n\nOSM/Overture undercount small business in lower-income and immigrant neighborhoods: exactly the areas the thesis flags as underserved. If the undercount is strong there and weak in Park Slope, the 'retail gap' is a data gap wearing a costume.\n\nDraw hexes at random within each income decile, and cross-stratify by foreign-born share (ACS) so borough / immigrant-neighborhood contrasts in the residual (QUESTIONS.md X3) can be separated from coverage bias. Fix n per stratum against the 5,000-call budget before spending anything.\n\nDONE 2026-09-02 — src/loci/validation/sample.py: NTILE(10) on median income among populated hexes, seeded random draw per decile, like-for-like 800 m straight-line counts for Google vs Overture/OSM/anchor. Foreign-born cross-stratification NOT possible yet: hex_demographics has no foreign-born column (add to ACS ingest first). Default plan: 200 hexes × 15 categories = 3,000 calls, inside the free tier."),
("E4 · Validation and Artifact", "Run Google Places ground-truth enumeration on sampled gap addresses", U, 5, "validation,critical,cost",
 "Enumerate ground truth for a stratified sample of gap ADDRESSES drawn from analysis.address_gaps. The sampling frame is the address itself, not a hex centroid standing in for one (D38); scope is Manhattan + Brooklyn (D48). Stratify by income_ratio decile — the continuous measure from analysis.address_demographics, not the retired binary income_class — and by borough, per QUESTIONS M1/D37. Fields: id/name/location/types ONLY; anything more reprices the SKU.\n\nRun on the FIXED harness or the money buys nothing (D50/D53):\n • includedPrimaryTypes, never includedTypes — loci assigns ONE category per place, so any-type matching double-counted a bakery-cafe under restaurant and cafe_bakery and a bodega under grocery and convenience.\n • n_ground_truth_at_cap recorded per row. Nearby Search (New) caps at 20 with no pagination, so a raw len(places) is right-censored; clinic hit the cap in 87.6% of sampled points. A capped row is a censored observation, not a count.\n • ground_truth_types persisted (primary-type histogram) so the per-category type audit stays reproducible.\n • radius_m = 649 written on every row — the gap screen's 800 m NETWORK threshold divided by NYC's measured circuity 1.233, derived in code, not a literal. This is what stops the validator and the screen from disagreeing by construction and needing a manual geometry/coverage split after the fact.\n\nEnforce the call budget in code. Populate analysis.coverage_validation keyed to address_id. Pre-D50 rows (clinic, fitness) are not usable as counts and must not be pooled with this run. Budget note from D50: a corrected 15-category re-run is ~14,850 calls (~$315) and needs a fresh budget approval before this ticket can start.\n\nReframed 2026-09-05 per CHECKPOINT D38 (address-level geography). Linear GTM-48.\nReframed 2026-09-09 (D38): sampling frame stated as gap addresses stratified on continuous income_ratio; fixed-harness terms (D50 primary-type filter + 20-cap column, D53 649 m per-row radius) written into scope."),
("E4 · Validation and Artifact", "DOHMH-anchored undercount calibration (address level)", U, 5, "validation,critical",
 "Within the food tier the true count is effectively known (DOHMH is a near-census). Measure the OSM/Overture discrepancy vs DOHMH per gap ADDRESS — keyed on address_id, aggregated to an address cluster only where a single ring is too thin to estimate a rate — then use that curve to estimate the expected undercount in the other tiers, where no census exists.\n\nThis is the strongest available mitigation and it costs nothing extra: it is the one calibration that does not consume Google budget, which is why it should run before GTM-48 rather than after.\n\nThe geometry must match the Google harness or the two calibration curves are not on the same footing: count within the same 649 m straight-line ring (800 m network / 1.233 measured circuity, D53) recorded per row as radius_m, and compare against the D50-corrected category definitions (primary-type semantics, one category per place). Join demographics tract -> address via analysis.address_demographics; never through hex_demographics, which the address screen does not read.\n\nReframed 2026-09-05 per CHECKPOINT D38 (address-level geography). Linear GTM-49.\nReframed 2026-09-09 (D38): unit is address_id; ring and category semantics aligned to the fixed harness (D50/D53) so this curve and the Google curve are comparable."),
("E4 · Validation and Artifact", "Coverage-bias chart", U, 2, "viz,critical",
 "Undercount rate by income decile, computed over gap ADDRESSES. Bin on income_ratio — the address's tract median household income over citywide MEAN household income, carried per address by analysis.address_demographics — not the retired binary income_class: D49 made the annotation continuous and MOE-gated, and a chart that re-imposes a threshold the screen no longer uses is measuring the threshold. NOT an appendix item.\n\nAddresses whose income_ratio is indeterminate at the tract MOE get their own visible band. Dropping them would bias exactly the low-income tail this chart exists to interrogate, since MOE is largest where the estimate is smallest.\n\nBuild it only from fixed-harness rows (D50). Pre-fix clinic and fitness rows are right-censored at the 20-result cap and semantically wrong (any-type matching), so a chart including them would plot the harness, not coverage.\n\nIf the finding survives this chart, the chart is the most persuasive artifact in the set. If it doesn't, this chart is how the project learns that honestly rather than shipping a bias as a finding.\n\nLoad the `dataviz` skill before writing chart code.\n\nReframed 2026-09-05 per CHECKPOINT D38 (address-level geography). Linear GTM-50.\nReframed 2026-09-09 (D38/D49): binned on continuous income_ratio from address_demographics, indeterminate shown as its own band, pre-D50 rows excluded."),
("E4 · Validation and Artifact", "Google type-map audit per category", U, 3, "validation,critical",
 "QUESTIONS.md M6, CHECKPOINT D29. The 2026-09-03 Google validation run showed raw survival rates (hardware 58%, fitness 29%, clinic 0%) were mostly measuring how much WIDER or NARROWER Google's included type is than loci's category definition, not measuring coverage — a cheap verification that would have de-risked the whole $-budgeted run if done first.\n\nKnown so far, by inspection only: hardware NARROWER (Google's hardware_store type excludes home_improvement_store, so 58% survival is an upper bound on the true hole, not the hole itself); fitness WIDER (gym/fitness_center sweeps in hotel/building gyms loci's bundle excludes); clinic MAXIMALLY wider (Google's doctor type includes every solo physician practice, which loci deliberately excludes — see the clinic re-anchor ticket).\n\nFor each of the 15 daily-needs categories (not just the three already sampled), enumerate the exact Google includedTypes used and compare against loci's category definition (registry.yaml + each adapter's category map) — narrower, wider, or aligned. Any survival rate for a category not yet audited this way should be treated as uninterpretable. Urgent: this is the cheap check that determines whether every future Google validation dollar buys a real answer.\n\nPushed to Linear 2026-09-05 as GTM-105 (High priority)."),
("E4 · Validation and Artifact", "Fitness anchor source for the ~20% true coverage hole", M, 3, "validation,universal,anchor",
 "QUESTIONS.md M7. D29 found a real ~21% [95% CI 10–37] true-coverage-hole rate for fitness after removing the geometry artifact (loci's canonical layer already had a business inside the 800m network radius but outside the validator's straight-line circle). Fitness has no anchor today — OSM/Overture/Foursquare are all general-purpose POI sources carrying the same undercount bias the project exists to correct for, and none is a near-census the way DOHMH is for food or SNAP is for grocery. D37 already flags fitness as the category most weakened by the demand annotation (on the frozen hex screen, 42% of window-rule fitness gaps sat below the low-income line, where the literature says gyms follow income rather than signalling a supply gap); per D49 that annotation is now continuous and MOE-gated and must be recomputed on addresses (GTM-110), but the reading does not change.\n\nCaveat on the headline number, added 2026-09-09: the ~21% came from a pre-D50 validation run that used any-type matching and an unrecorded 20-result cap, and D50 states plainly that existing fitness rows are not usable as counts. Treat ~21% as the motivating anomaly, not the measurement — the size of the hole has to be re-established on the fixed harness (GTM-48) at address level. That does not weaken the case for finding an anchor; fitness having no near-census is true regardless of the rate.\n\nCandidates to evaluate: a NYS business registry cut for fitness/gym SIC/NAICS codes, DOHMH (if it licenses any fitness-adjacent facility), or state gym/health-club contract-law registration (verify whether NY requires it and whether the file is bulk-accessible).\n\nWhichever anchor is chosen must land in the same staging.poi contract the other anchors use, so the address-level screen (analysis.address_gaps) picks it up without a fitness-specific code path.\n\nReframed 2026-09-09 (D38/D49/D50): hex framing removed from the demand-annotation line; the ~21% is flagged as a pre-fixed-harness figure that GTM-48 must re-measure on addresses.\n\nMoved from E1 to E4 and pushed to Linear 2026-09-05 as GTM-106."),
("E4 · Validation and Artifact", "Clinic re-anchor to licensed urgent care", M, 3, "validation,nyc,anchor",
 "CHECKPOINT D30. The clinic gap is currently unfalsifiable by construction: `sources/universal/foursquare_places.py` deliberately excludes solo doctor's offices from the clinic category, but Google's `doctor` type includes every one of them and saturates at the API cap — 20 results with no pagination, hit at 87.6% of sampled points (D50) — so ANY clinic gap will show a 0% true-hole rate whether or not a real gap exists.\n\nD50 hardened this rather than solving it: the cap is now recorded per row (n_ground_truth_at_cap), so saturation is visible instead of being silently read as a count, and clinic was REMOVED from the Google type map entirely because no Table A primary type isolates NAICS 621493. That makes this ticket the only path back — Google cannot validate clinic at any radius, including the 649 m ring (D53).\n\nFix: re-anchor clinic to licensed URGENT CARE only (NYS Article 28 facility licensing, or the DOHMH health-facility list), landed in staging.poi like the other anchors so the address-level screen picks it up unchanged. Until this lands, clinic stays excluded from headline gap claims AND from the demand annotation (D30/D49) — this ticket is what unblocks re-including it.\n\nReframed 2026-09-09 (D38/D50): per-hex saturation language replaced with the per-sampled-point figure and the recorded-cap column; states that clinic is off the Google map, so this is the only route.\n\nMoved from E1 to E4 and pushed to Linear 2026-09-05 as GTM-107."),
("E4 · Validation and Artifact", "ZBP per-source overcount attribution", H, 5, "validation,critical",
 "CHECKPOINT D40 / QUESTIONS M9. Loci POI counts exceed ZBP establishment counts by a median 2-8x in most categories, concentrated in license-registry-anchored categories (restaurant 3.2x, cafe 3.5x, hair 2.8x, nails 8.0x, bar 6.0x) plus fitness 5.9x, while OSM/Overture-only categories sit near 1 (childcare 0.85, clinic 0.95, laundry 1.3, pharmacy 1.5). Every revealed-spacing reach value in a suspect category is biased tight because it is computed against an inflated denominator.\n\nScope: (1) rerun `loci zbp-compare` split by source (DOHMH / NYS DOS / NYS SLA / OSM / Overture) to attribute which feed drives each category's overcount; (2) decide whether SLA and NYS DOS anchors need an active-license filter like DOHMH's; (3) add ZCTA polygons for a proper POI→ZIP join, replacing the current majority-vote hex→ZIP crosswalk. High priority: conditions the D41 reach calibration in the affected categories.\n\nPushed to Linear 2026-09-05 as GTM-108."),
("E4 · Validation and Artifact", "Validator: network distance or circuity correction", L, 2, "validation,method",
 "QUESTIONS.md M8. D29's GEOMETRY-artifact category (Google found the business AND loci's canonical layer already had it, just outside the validator's straight-line radius but inside the true 800m network-walk radius — median network distance for these hexes ~850m, IQR 830–930) exists because the validator compares Google results against a straight-line circle while the gap screen itself thresholds on NETWORK distance (analysis.hex_poi_distance). The two geographies disagree by construction, which is why every validation result needed a manual geometry/coverage split after the fact.\n\nTwo fixes to evaluate: (a) have the validator query analysis.hex_poi_distance directly instead of a straight-line radius, so the comparison uses the same geography as the screen; or (b) keep the straight-line radius but apply a circuity correction (NYC's street network runs ~1.25–1.3x straight-line distance, so an 800m network threshold corresponds to roughly a 620–640m straight-line radius). Either removes the need to hand-split future validation runs. Low priority: the current manual split (D29) already works, this just automates it."),
("E4 · Validation and Artifact", "PMTiles export + MapLibre map", H, 5, "viz",
 "H3 choropleth, static hosting, no server. Layer toggle between DNCI (sequential) and residual (DIVERGING — zero is a real midpoint, not an arbitrary one).\n\nLoad the `dataviz` skill before writing chart code.\n\nCANCELED 2026-09-05 (CHECKPOINT D38, Linear GTM-51): hex geography retired; PMTiles tiles the H3 grid by construction — no address-level equivalent."),
("E4 · Validation and Artifact", "Bivariate transit × residual map", H, 3, "viz",
 "3×3 bivariate palette. This is the map that states the thesis in one image: where transit-rich and underserved overlap.\n\nCANCELED 2026-09-05 (CHECKPOINT D38, Linear GTM-52): hex geography retired; maps the abandoned E3 hex residual against transit, and D37 separately found transit must NOT be used as an expected-supply covariate."),
("E4 · Validation and Artifact", "Per-category radar small multiples for top gap clusters", M, 3, "viz",
 "15-spoke radar per top address-level gap cluster (analysis.address_gaps clustering, D44). Answers 'what specifically is missing here — grocery, or salons?' Turns a score into an actionable observation.\n\nReframed 2026-09-05 per CHECKPOINT D38 (address-level geography). Linear GTM-53."),
("E4 · Validation and Artifact", "Scatter: residual@t0 vs growth t0→t1", H, 2, "viz",
 "Fitted line + CI, borough-colored. The visual statement of P2.\n\nCANCELED 2026-09-05 (CHECKPOINT D38, Linear GTM-54): hex geography retired; visual statement of P2, the causal growth thesis already empirically REJECTED — no address-level equivalent."),
("E4 · Validation and Artifact", "4–6 neighborhood evidence cards", H, 5, "artifact,narrative",
 "Narrative + inset map per selected address-level gap cluster (analysis.address_gaps clustering, D44). Does the mechanism look real on the ground? This is what converts a score into something a reader believes.\n\nReframed 2026-09-05 per CHECKPOINT D38 (address-level geography). Linear GTM-55."),
("E4 · Validation and Artifact", "Methodology memo", H, 5, "docs,artifact",
 "The document a skeptic attacks and you defend. What it now documents is the ADDRESS-level gap screen (analysis.address_gaps + analysis.address_demographics, D38) — the hex tables are frozen history and the memo should not read as though they are the product.\n\nMust state plainly:\n • The unit and the rule: a per-category reach screen over residential addresses, reach values from reach_tiers.yaml and the ranking continuous (D41), scoped to Manhattan + Brooklyn (D48). Output is GRADED — every lead carries a grade, none is filtered out — so the memo must show the full ranked surface rather than a shortlist that hides the marginal cases.\n • The reach values come from revealed SPACING, i.e. historical supply, so a category the whole city under-supplies looks like it 'naturally' spaces wide. That is the screen's central circularity and it belongs in the memo, not in a footnote.\n • Coverage bias (§7.1): what the fixed-harness validation showed, which categories are still uninterpretable (clinic, D30/D50), and the ZBP overcount in license-anchored categories (D40) that biases those reach values tight.\n • The demand annotation is continuous, MOE-gated, and NON-FILTERING (D37/D49), with the X6 disclaimer in the reader's own words: it says the literature expects lower discretionary demand here, never that the gap is false.\n • The rejected causal thesis, kept and labelled: the residual design reduced but did not eliminate endogeneity (§7.2), no instrument was used, pre-trends broke, and the retail-gap-predicts-growth coefficient came out wrong-signed (§0/D1). It ships as an honest negative result, never as a claim the product rests on.\n\nClaiming cleaner identification than exists is the fastest way to lose an investment reader.\n\nReframed 2026-09-09 (D38): the memo defends the address-level screen; the hex residual and growth regression are retained only as the stated negative result."),
("E4 · Validation and Artifact", "Fresh-clone reproducibility test", H, 3, "infra,qa",
 "Acceptance criterion C: fresh clone → `make nyc` → outputs in <30 min on a laptop. Pinned deps, documented lineage.\n\nAlso assert loci/score/ contains no NYC-specific column names — the portability guarantee from CONTEXT.md §10."),
("E4 · Validation and Artifact", "SHIP W4: public artifact", U, 2, "milestone",
 "Week-4 exit criterion, in its post-D38 form: the public ADDRESS webmap (the address gap layer served by webmap/, per D38/D44) + the methodology memo + the address-cluster evidence cards. It is NOT the retired H3 PMTiles choropleth — that deliverable was cancelled with the hex geography (GTM-51).\n\nScope is Manhattan + Brooklyn (D48), and the artifact must show a graded surface: every address lead carries its grade and its caveat text, and nothing is filtered out of the published view. A public map that quietly shows only the top slice is a filter the reader cannot see, which is exactly what D48 forbids.\n\nLoad the `dataviz` skill before writing chart code.\n\nReframed 2026-09-09 (D38): the shipped map is the address layer, not the hex choropleth; graded-not-filtered stated per D48."),
